

[38;5;231m⏺[39m On the concern I flagged: an app-generated id is anonymous, so the PII and shared-machine worries largely fall away.
  It becomes a plain durability question instead, which is the next thing I need from you.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persists" has three different meanings in a browser, and they differ in how long the id lives and whether
  your backend can see it without you doing anything.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — one id per browser, survives tab and browser close, lives until cleared. Buys the longest-lived
    thread across visits and forms. Costs: never expires on its own, and the id only reaches your server if code
    explicitly puts it in the request. Easy to change later.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — one id per tab, gone when the tab closes. Buys a clean per-sitting scope. Costs: two tabs are two
    different "users", and nothing correlates across visits — which undercuts "other forms will need it later" if those
    forms are reached in a new tab.
  - [1mCookie[0m — same durability options, but sent automatically on every request to your origin. Buys a server that can
    join the id to the login with zero client work. Costs: it rides along on every request whether you want it or not,
    and it's the one choice here with a consent/compliance dimension. Hardest of the three to walk back once a backend
    depends on it.

  [1mMy recommendation is [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m It matches "persists and works across the app" most directly, the app is a static
  page whose API call is still a stub so there's no server to auto-join anything yet, and if you later decide the server
  needs the id ambiently, moving to a cookie is a contained change.
